WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:02.330
- [Mark] Damian.

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:03.498
- Yes.

00:00:03.498 --> 00:00:04.810
- [Mark] The last time we spoke was, what?

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:06.210
Maybe three, four months ago,

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.190
something like that?
- Yeah.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.120
- [Mark] And your story was incredible.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.026
- Thank you.

00:00:10.026 --> 00:00:12.170
- [Mark] You told the story.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:15.280
I see a lot of comments
saying it's a bullshit story.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.580
(chuckling)

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.860
A six-month-old baby
crawling out of a lake

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.200
to go back to the house it came from.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.033
- Yeah.

00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:23.530
- [Mark] Is a little bit far-fetched,

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:24.780
but do you say it's still true?

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:25.670
- Yeah.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:30.210
I mean, they told me
separately, mother and father,

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.500
like they were talking
about the weather, you know.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.950
And they each told me
on different occasions

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.753
and they hadn't been together, for

00:00:37.753 --> 00:00:41.040
you know, for many years, you know.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.537
Last time they were together, I was,

00:00:43.537 --> 00:00:48.270
I was probably like, maybe
two years old, if that.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.210
And so they hadn't been together

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.260
for that long, you know and,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.390
- [Mark] So you were at least,

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.700
at the very most, you were two years old.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.615
Sometimes stories get into papers.

00:00:56.615 --> 00:00:57.620
- Yeah, yeah.
- [Mark] Sometimes stories get

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:58.670
exaggerated or embellished.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.250
I don't think I've told
a story about my youth

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.599
that wasn't exaggerated
in some form, right?

00:01:03.599 --> 00:01:05.100
- Somewhat, I guess.
- It's just human nature.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.630
- But from what they told me,

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.990
I was six months old,
had Cholic, coughing,

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.400
and I was a buzzkill.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.950
And so they were trying to fix,

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.580
and were just having so many
problems with me coughing,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.990
and them having to pay attention to me,

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:24.060
that they decided that, "we
ain't doing this no more.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.260
We're driving down the road

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.710
and we're tossing this little

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.059
helter-skelter hellion,
whatever", you know.

00:01:31.059 --> 00:01:31.892
- [Mark] For those that haven't seen

00:01:31.892 --> 00:01:33.890
your first video, your dad was a biker.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:34.723
- Yeah.

00:01:34.723 --> 00:01:36.070
- [Mark] Hell's Angel, right?
- Hell's Angels, yes sir.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:37.250
- [Mark] And your mom was?

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.820
- She was a biker slut. (chuckling)

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:41.653
I mean...

00:01:41.653 --> 00:01:43.330
- [Mark] So your mom actually,

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:44.800
another story you told was that

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.280
your mom murdered a guy
in a hardware store?

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.113
- Yeah.

00:01:47.113 --> 00:01:48.750
- [Mark] And that's true as well?

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:49.583
- I watched that,

00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:52.270
I physically watched
that with my own eyes.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:53.103
- [Mark] How old were you?

00:01:53.103 --> 00:01:54.930
- I was like, five or six years old.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.143
He cut us off,

00:01:57.138 --> 00:01:59.640
and ran a red light.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.730
My mom ran a red light chasing him,

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.290
because when he cut us off,
she slammed on the brakes,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.400
and we were in an old Ford pickup truck,

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.240
no seat belts, and I busted my nose

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.950
against the dash, bleeding
and stuff, you know.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.540
So that pissed her off.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.230
And then, so she goes and runs
like, a couple red lights,

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.350
you know, chasing this dude down.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.400
He pulls into a hardware store,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.307
and she pulls in after him and she's like,

00:02:22.307 --> 00:02:24.030
"Wait in the fucking truck", you know.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.537
And I'm like, "Yeah, right", you know.

00:02:26.537 --> 00:02:28.520
"I wanna see what's going on", you know,

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.340
I was a little kid, you
know, Curious George.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:35.170
And so I got in, got out,
and went in after her

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.630
and just saw her just mangle
this guy, like literally,

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:41.180
until his body stopped moving,

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.190
and he stopped crying. (chuckling)

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:44.350
- [Mark] So you had a rough childhood,

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:45.380
it sounds like.
- Yeah.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:46.730
- [Mark] With your parents.
- Yeah.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:48.390
- [Mark] What's going on today now?

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.760
It's been several months.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.640
- Yeah, I mean, I...

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:53.730
- [Mark] You using fentanyl?

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.070
- Yeah.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:55.903
- [Mark] Still?

00:02:55.903 --> 00:02:57.110
- And yeah,

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:00.150
and I went to detox though, recently.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.960
And I was really proud
of myself, you know.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.300
But then I was kicking suboxone

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.123
while I was in the rehab place, Arena B.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:13.410
Beautiful place, you know, awesome place.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:18.410
And for some reason though,
my psyche flipped out on me.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.180
Like, I went wingnut on myself,

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.370
and just up and left the place.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:30.370
You know, knowing damn
good and well that I had a,

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.470
a better future ahead of me in this place,

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:35.737
but for some reason, my
psyche was telling me,

00:03:35.737 --> 00:03:38.790
"you're incarcerated. You're locked up.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:40.560
You need to get out of here.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.160
We can't do this locked
up thing", you know.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.270
And so for some reason,
instead of walking out of

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.580
the front gate, which I could've done,

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.973
I packed up my stuff and
I jumped the fucking wall.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:56.860
But the second that I got on
the other side of the wall,

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.960
I like, started crying and
kicking myself in the ass,

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.423
like wondering, what
was I doing? You know.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:07.190
I really didn't understand,
you know. (chuckling)

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:08.733
- [Mark] What do you
think that's all about?

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.380
- I don't know.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:17.040
I mean, I wanna say,
psychologically speaking,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.050
it's just the primal side of me, you know,

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:22.280
not wanting to be incarcerated,

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.940
even though I wasn't incarcerated.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.280
I was in a place that was nurturing me

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.250
and taking care of me and helping me.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:30.550
- [Mark] The place was great.
- Yeah.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:34.380
But for some reason, because
I couldn't walk outside

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.490
of the facility, 'cause I
like to walk a lot, you know.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:40.200
Just see things, you know.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.983
And because I couldn't do that,

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.589
I think that was the main issue with me.

00:04:45.589 --> 00:04:47.410
I'm not really sure, though.

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:50.110
- [Mark] Do you think
it could've been just

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:54.123
your almost subconscious belief
of what you deserve in life?

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:56.163
- Could be, but,

00:04:56.163 --> 00:04:56.996
- [Mark] And then getting clean

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:57.829
and getting your life together,

00:04:57.829 --> 00:05:01.820
or the fear of that, was
too much to deal with?

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.200
- That could very well be it.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.947
I never even thought about that one.

00:05:04.947 --> 00:05:06.330
- [Mark] And you self-sabotage?

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:07.940
- Yeah.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.360
- [Mark] Because I've talked
to so many people now.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.100
I've heard, whether I bring it up or not,

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.050
I hear these stories and it's like,

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.890
it seems to me like they're
self-sabotaging themselves.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.550
Their own forward progress gets derailed.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:20.772
- Yeah.
- [Mark] By themselves.

00:05:20.772 --> 00:05:24.100
- And then I get thrown
back into the shit.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:24.933
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:05:24.933 --> 00:05:28.080
- You know and it's like, I
don't wanna be there, you know.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:31.650
I believe I, with I mean,
with the good that I do

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.130
for other people and stuff,

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:36.020
and the good that I'd
like to do constantly,

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.259
I believe I deserve a better life.

00:05:38.259 --> 00:05:40.020
- [Mark] You seem intelligent,

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.930
more intelligent than most,

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:42.980
- Thank you.
- [Mark] By a long shot,

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:47.437
and self-aware enough to say,

00:05:47.437 --> 00:05:51.490
"I'm making mistakes and I
need to not make mistakes."

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:53.270
But you're still, here you are still.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:54.120
- Yeah.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.150
- Well, this is a really big
helping hand for me, though,

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:01.840
right now, because now,
I mean what I'm gonna do

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.700
with this is, first and foremost,
buy me some new clothes.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:07.790
'Cause I've been wearing these clothes

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:09.130
for like, four months.

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.400
- [Mark] Really?

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.880
- And then, I'm going to
Cedars-Sinai for their detox.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.060
- [Mark] Oh, you are?
- Yeah.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:16.610
- [Mark] That's great.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:19.101
So how do you get into that program?

00:06:19.101 --> 00:06:21.520
Does it cost you anything?

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:23.443
- Medi-Cal pays for it.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:26.350
- [Mark] And you've been
through rehab how many times?

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:27.183
- Once.

00:06:27.183 --> 00:06:28.070
- [Mark] Just that one time?
- Yeah.

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:29.040
- [Mark] So this'll be the second time?

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:29.873
- Yeah.

00:06:29.873 --> 00:06:30.933
- [Mark] Good for you.

00:06:32.264 --> 00:06:36.340
- I've always wanted to, and could just,

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:38.730
in the past, I always beat myself up

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:43.253
from my past, you know, like with the,

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:46.910
I talked to you about Thomas West, right?

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:47.860
- [Mark] Yes.
- Yeah,

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:50.830
about that particular stuff, you know?

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:51.730
- [Mark] That part, nobody doubts,

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:54.180
because I think there's
newspaper articles.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.500
- Yeah.
- [Mark] Showing that.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:56.333
- Yeah.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:58.233
And it's like,

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.580
for some reason, I was
always beating myself up,

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.100
seeming to think that I
was at fault, you know.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:08.340
- [Mark] To me, as an outsider

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:12.330
